<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Kimigayo March (Kōzō Yoshimoto)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">adapted as, with Isawa Shūji's &quot;Defense of the Japanese Empire&quot;</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Recording by Kiyoshiro Imawano on Fuyu no Jujika</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1999</span><span class="sg-chain-h">cover version</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Anonymous waka in the Kokin Wakashū</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">c. 920</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the words</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Kimigayo, first melody by John William Fenton</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1869-1870</span><span class="sg-chain-h">elements of its arrangement used in the 1880 melody</span></li><li><a href="/songs/god-save-the-king">God Save the King</a><span class="sg-chain-h">the words may have been chosen for their likeness to it</span></li></ul></section>
